\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {II}{\ignorespaces Primary Bayes-factor sensitivity to the bounce prior, evaluated for a mock SPHEREx detection at $f_{\rm  NL}=-4.375$ ($\sigma =0.7$) at fixed baseline GR ($\sigma _{\rm  GR}=0.5$). The $\sigma _{\rm  theory}=1.0$ Gaussian row is the recommended physically motivated headline (BF~$\sim 10$ at broad multifield $[-15,+15]$, BF~$\sim 4$ at narrow $[-5,+5]$), encompassing both the Cai et al.\ and Li \& Brandenberger values plus the full $\epsilon $-correction range. The delta-prior row is reported only as the theoretical-maximum upper bound; any finite theoretical uncertainty in the $f_{\rm  NL}=-35/8$ value monotonically reduces the Bayes factor (Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}). $^a$The 8--11 spread on this row reflects the GR-marginalization variation across the four scenarios of Table~\ref {tab:gr} at fixed \emph  {narrow} multifield competitor prior $[-5,+5]$; this is the same competitor prior that produces the $7.9$--$10.9$ ``BF vs.\ Tuned'' column of Table~\ref {tab:gr}, and the two tables are numerically consistent at $\sigma _{\rm  GR}=0.5$ (Table~\ref {tab:bayes} row 4 = 9.4 = Table~\ref {tab:gr} row 2 BF-vs-Tuned). The recommended $\sigma _{\rm  theory}=1.0$ headline of BF~$\sim 10$ is approximately constant under the same GR variation. $^b$The $\sim \!17$ row reports the delta-bounce-prior Bayes factor at the broad-multifield competitor prior $[-15,+15]$ and fixed baseline GR ($\sigma _{\rm  GR}=0.5$); this is the delta-prior endpoint of the competitor-prior sensitivity scan in Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}. The abstract envelope $\sim \!10$--$17$ now brackets the recommended-prior lower bound (BF~$\sim \!10$, row 1) up to the delta-prior maximum at broad multifield (BF~$\sim \!17$, row 5), with broader bounce priors giving \emph  {smaller} Bayes factors. \textbf  {v1.7.35 R-next-c-MAJ-1 correction}: prior versions of this caption + the inline 2-row Bayes-factor tabular preceding this caption (immediately before \S  \ref {sec:bayesian}'s closing paragraph) reported BF~$\sim 8$ at the recommended baseline and BF~$\sim 6$ at the narrow-competitor column; direct scipy.stats.norm recompute of the closed-form Gaussian-prior/uniform-competitor formula gives BF=9.80 (broad column) and BF=4.01 (narrow column) at the $\sigma _{\rm  theory}=1.0$ row, which round to $\sim 10$ and $\sim 4$ respectively. The delta-prior rows (BF=7.00 narrow, BF=17.10 broad) were already exact and are unchanged.}}{11}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:bayes}{{II}{11}{Primary Bayes-factor sensitivity to the bounce prior, evaluated for a mock SPHEREx detection at $\fnl =-4.375$ ($\sigma =0.7$) at fixed baseline GR ($\sigma _{\rm GR}=0.5$). The $\sigma _{\rm theory}=1.0$ Gaussian row is the recommended physically motivated headline (BF~$\sim 10$ at broad multifield $[-15,+15]$, BF~$\sim 4$ at narrow $[-5,+5]$), encompassing both the Cai et al.\ and Li \& Brandenberger values plus the full $\epsilon $-correction range. The delta-prior row is reported only as the theoretical-maximum upper bound; any finite theoretical uncertainty in the $\fnl =-35/8$ value monotonically reduces the Bayes factor (Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}). $^a$The 8--11 spread on this row reflects the GR-marginalization variation across the four scenarios of Table~\ref {tab:gr} at fixed \emph {narrow} multifield competitor prior $[-5,+5]$; this is the same competitor prior that produces the $7.9$--$10.9$ ``BF vs.\ Tuned'' column of Table~\ref {tab:gr}, and the two tables are numerically consistent at $\sigma _{\rm GR}=0.5$ (Table~\ref {tab:bayes} row 4 = 9.4 = Table~\ref {tab:gr} row 2 BF-vs-Tuned). The recommended $\sigma _{\rm theory}=1.0$ headline of BF~$\sim 10$ is approximately constant under the same GR variation. $^b$The $\sim \!17$ row reports the delta-bounce-prior Bayes factor at the broad-multifield competitor prior $[-15,+15]$ and fixed baseline GR ($\sigma _{\rm GR}=0.5$); this is the delta-prior endpoint of the competitor-prior sensitivity scan in Sec.~\ref {sec:bayesian}. The abstract envelope $\sim \!10$--$17$ now brackets the recommended-prior lower bound (BF~$\sim \!10$, row 1) up to the delta-prior maximum at broad multifield (BF~$\sim \!17$, row 5), with broader bounce priors giving \emph {smaller} Bayes factors. \textbf {v1.7.35 R-next-c-MAJ-1 correction}: prior versions of this caption + the inline 2-row Bayes-factor tabular preceding this caption (immediately before \S \ref {sec:bayesian}'s closing paragraph) reported BF~$\sim 8$ at the recommended baseline and BF~$\sim 6$ at the narrow-competitor column; direct scipy.stats.norm recompute of the closed-form Gaussian-prior/uniform-competitor formula gives BF=9.80 (broad column) and BF=4.01 (narrow column) at the $\sigma _{\rm theory}=1.0$ row, which round to $\sim 10$ and $\sim 4$ respectively. The delta-prior rows (BF=7.00 narrow, BF=17.10 broad) were already exact and are unchanged}{table.2}{}}
